{
  "dom_id": "step-q120228",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(x+3)y$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(3-x)y$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/9051213882bcb506ddae46e0a6a79c8cd03a10eadd89e0007f895f68b00c33de.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=3$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=(x+3)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=(3-x)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=3$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
